docs/engineering/PROJECT_GUIDE.md:5: ## Architecture
docs/engineering/PROJECT_GUIDE.md:6: 
docs/engineering/PROJECT_GUIDE.md:7: - Transport and UI live in `app/` (routes, layouts, Server Actions) and `components/` (`layout/`, `marketplace/`, `ui/`).
docs/engineering/PROJECT_GUIDE.md:8: - Domain types live in `types/index.ts`.
docs/engineering/PROJECT_GUIDE.md:9: - The data boundary is `lib/data.ts`: catalog and quote reads go through Supabase, with an automatic fallback to `lib/mock.ts` when `NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL` is missing or still the example placeholder. Do not query Supabase from components.
docs/engineering/PROJECT_GUIDE.md:10: - `lib/supabase/client.ts`, `lib/supabase/server.ts`, and `lib/supabase/proxy.ts` are clients only.
docs/engineering/PROJECT_GUIDE.md:11: - The cart is client-only Zustand in `lib/store/carrito.ts`, persisted in `localStorage`.
docs/engineering/PROJECT_GUIDE.md:12: - Quote creation resolves provider and price on the server. Never trust prices or provider ids sent by the client.
docs/engineering/PROJECT_GUIDE.md:13: - Authorization is Postgres Row Level Security in `supabase/schema.sql`. UI checks are not the authorization layer.
docs/engineering/PROJECT_GUIDE.md:14: - Buyer flows: `app/(marketplace)/` catalog, cart, quotes. Provider flows: `app/(marketplace)/proveedor/`. Auth: `app/(auth)/`.
docs/engineering/PROJECT_GUIDE.md:15: 
docs/engineering/PROJECT_GUIDE.md:16: ## Gate commands
docs/engineering/PROJECT_GUIDE.md:17: 
docs/engineering/PROJECT_GUIDE.md:18: | Command | What it checks | Shared state |
docs/engineering/PROJECT_GUIDE.md:19: |---|---|---|
docs/engineering/PROJECT_GUIDE.md:20: | `npm run test` | Vitest unit tests (`tests/`). Local only; no Supabase. | Does not mutate shared remote state. |
docs/engineering/PROJECT_GUIDE.md:21: | `npm run lint` | ESLint. | Local. |
docs/engineering/PROJECT_GUIDE.md:22: | `npm run typecheck` | Generates Next.js route types and checks TypeScript. | Writes `*.tsbuildinfo` (gitignored). |
docs/engineering/PROJECT_GUIDE.md:23: | `npm run build` | Production Next.js build. | Writes `.next/`. |
docs/engineering/PROJECT_GUIDE.md:24: | `npm run dev` | Dev server on port 3000. | Binds port 3000. |
docs/engineering/PROJECT_GUIDE.md:25: 
docs/engineering/PROJECT_GUIDE.md:26: A skipped test is not a pass. Do not claim a command passed unless you ran it and saw it pass.
docs/engineering/PROJECT_GUIDE.md:27: 
docs/engineering/PROJECT_GUIDE.md:28: ## Shared resources
docs/engineering/PROJECT_GUIDE.md:29: 
docs/engineering/PROJECT_GUIDE.md:30: Parallel workers share this checkout. In one fan-out, assign each of these to at most one worker:
docs/engineering/PROJECT_GUIDE.md:31: 
docs/engineering/PROJECT_GUIDE.md:32: - `npm run dev` (port 3000)
docs/engineering/PROJECT_GUIDE.md:33: - `npm run build` and `npm run typecheck` (they write compiler output)
docs/engineering/PROJECT_GUIDE.md:34: - Applying `supabase/schema.sql` or `supabase/seed.sql` (one shared Supabase project)
docs/engineering/PROJECT_GUIDE.md:35: - `npx vercel deploy` (production)
docs/engineering/PROJECT_GUIDE.md:36: 
docs/engineering/PROJECT_GUIDE.md:37: `npm run test` and `npm run lint` do not touch Supabase. Still do not run two copies of the same gate at once on this checkout.
docs/engineering/PROJECT_GUIDE.md:38: 
docs/engineering/PROJECT_GUIDE.md:39: Do not add npm dependencies unless the approved plan includes them. Do not commit `.env`, `.env.local`, or any secret. `.env.example` is the only env file that belongs in git.
docs/engineering/PROJECT_GUIDE.md:40: 
docs/engineering/PROJECT_GUIDE.md:41: UI, layout, routing, client state, or rendered-data changes need a browser pass of the affected flow before the work is called complete. If no browser is available, use the closest substitute (`npm run test`, or a request against the dev server) and say what was not verified.
docs/engineering/PROJECT_GUIDE.md:42: 
docs/engineering/CURSOR_ORCHESTRATION.md:20: # PHASE 0 - CLASSIFY THE REQUEST
docs/engineering/CURSOR_ORCHESTRATION.md:21: 
docs/engineering/CURSOR_ORCHESTRATION.md:22: - A. explanation or research: answer normally.
docs/engineering/CURSOR_ORCHESTRATION.md:23: - B. trivial change: you may implement it directly unless the user asks for orchestration.
docs/engineering/CURSOR_ORCHESTRATION.md:24: - C or D. non-trivial or architectural work: use the workflow below.
docs/engineering/CURSOR_ORCHESTRATION.md:25: 
docs/engineering/CURSOR_ORCHESTRATION.md:26: When unsure between B and C, treat it as C.
docs/engineering/CURSOR_ORCHESTRATION.md:27: 
docs/engineering/CURSOR_ORCHESTRATION.md:28: # PHASE 1 - DISCOVERY
docs/engineering/CURSOR_ORCHESTRATION.md:29: 
docs/engineering/CURSOR_ORCHESTRATION.md:30: Inspect the relevant code, patterns, constraints, regression surfaces, and gate commands. Check `git status` for unrelated uncommitted work. Do not modify production code and do not spawn implementation workers.
docs/engineering/CURSOR_ORCHESTRATION.md:31: 
docs/engineering/CURSOR_ORCHESTRATION.md:32: # PHASE 2 - PLAN
docs/engineering/CURSOR_ORCHESTRATION.md:33: 
docs/engineering/CURSOR_ORCHESTRATION.md:34: Produce a plan with goal, current state, proposed design, affected files, bounded tasks, dependencies, parallelizable work, risks, numbered acceptance criteria, a verification strategy, and preconditions. Each task names an owner, the files it owns, and the criteria it covers.
docs/engineering/CURSOR_ORCHESTRATION.md:35: 
docs/engineering/CURSOR_ORCHESTRATION.md:36: # PHASE 3 - APPROVAL GATE
docs/engineering/CURSOR_ORCHESTRATION.md:37: 
docs/engineering/CURSOR_ORCHESTRATION.md:38: After presenting the plan, stop. Do not edit implementation files, spawn workers, code, migrate, or install dependencies. Wait for an explicit approval such as "Approved", "Go ahead", or "Implement it".
docs/engineering/CURSOR_ORCHESTRATION.md:39: 
docs/engineering/CURSOR_ORCHESTRATION.md:40: # PHASE 4 - FREEZE THE SPECIFICATION
docs/engineering/CURSOR_ORCHESTRATION.md:41: 
docs/engineering/CURSOR_ORCHESTRATION.md:42: After approval, behavior, architecture, task boundaries, constraints, and acceptance criteria are frozen. Save the plan to `docs/implementation-plans/<YYYY-MM-DD>-<short-slug>.md` and reference that path in every worker and verifier packet.
docs/engineering/CURSOR_ORCHESTRATION.md:43: 
docs/engineering/CURSOR_ORCHESTRATION.md:44: If the plan cannot work, stop the affected work, explain the constraint, and ask for approval before changing behavior or architecture.
docs/engineering/CURSOR_ORCHESTRATION.md:45: 
docs/engineering/CURSOR_ORCHESTRATION.md:46: # PHASE 5 - DECOMPOSE WORK
docs/engineering/CURSOR_ORCHESTRATION.md:47: 
docs/engineering/CURSOR_ORCHESTRATION.md:48: Assign each task to the agent whose role fits it.
docs/engineering/CURSOR_ORCHESTRATION.md:49: 
docs/engineering/CURSOR_ORCHESTRATION.md:50: # PHASE 6 - MINIMIZE WORKER OVERLAP
docs/engineering/CURSOR_ORCHESTRATION.md:51: 
docs/engineering/CURSOR_ORCHESTRATION.md:52: Parallel workers must own disjoint files. They share the checkout unless you request an isolated worktree, and an isolated worktree is built from committed history. If two workers must touch the same file, run them sequentially.
docs/engineering/CURSOR_ORCHESTRATION.md:53: 
docs/engineering/CURSOR_ORCHESTRATION.md:54: # PHASE 7 - DELEGATE WITH COMPLETE TASK PACKETS
docs/engineering/CURSOR_ORCHESTRATION.md:55: 
docs/engineering/CURSOR_ORCHESTRATION.md:56: Every packet includes objective, approved design, exact file scope, relevant files, the acceptance criteria verbatim, constraints, dependencies, out of scope, and the exact validation commands, including which shared-resource gates that worker may run.
docs/engineering/CURSOR_ORCHESTRATION.md:57: 
docs/engineering/CURSOR_ORCHESTRATION.md:58: # PHASE 8 - PARALLEL EXECUTION
